Mark sawatka is the General Secretary of  the PCS Union and he joins us now from  West Wales thank you so much for talking  to us this morning  um you met now I know you're in Wales  but you met remotely with Jeremy Quinn  Minister for the cabinet office this  morning what came of that meeting  uh well good morning to you and your  viewers  um well I'm sad to say the meeting was a  complete fast  we were invited to the meeting after the  Prime Minister indicated a few days ago  that he wanted constructive talks to try  to avert industrial action in the public  sector  um what we got this morning was a  meeting with the minister who confirmed  that he recognized the civil service has  had the lowest pay rate anywhere in the  economy we have some of the lowest rates  of pay 40 000 people using food banks 45  000 people in the Civil Service claiming  inward benefits and yesterday tens of  thousands of civil servants got an email  to say their pay would go up because the  national minimum wage was increasing  because their pay is so low and yet what  he told us today is there's no money on  the table the government is not  considering alleviating this crisis by  any means now including one-off payments  or finding extra money all he will talk  about is 2023's pay and all of the  unions present made it clear that if  that remains the government's position  industrial action is likely to escalate  because we have to do something about  this poverty now and the minister  confirmed this morning that he has no  intention of doing anything about it now  nothing for 2022 but what did they offer  for 2023 pay 23 24. nothing what they  told us is that they might try to be  creative in 2023 they listen to what we  said but we'd have to have discussions  at the appropriate time and the reason  why that is so incredibly frustrating  and outrageous actually is that the  minister knows that he has people  involved in deciding Asylum claims  paying Universal Credit keeping our  justice system running uh keeping the  department for Education offsted museums  and galleries right across the economy  he knows those people are claiming  benefits uh really really struggling and  have a crisis of mental health issues  and physical issues because their pay is  so low and yet he's saying I'm not doing  anything about it and that means the  government seems to be choosing whether  it's in health education or the Civil  Service to say we are prepared to accept  this level of poverty and the disruption  that is going on and I think most people  hopefully will take the view that is an  appalling thing for the government of  this country to do  okay well let me put to you what a  camera office spokesperson has said  following your meeting with Jeremy Quinn  the meeting included discussions to help  ensure fair and affordable public sector  pay settlements we regret the PCS  decision to call further strike action  but discussions will continue and we  have comprehensive plans in place to  keep essential Services running and to  minimize disruption so let's break this  down first of all what's your reaction  to to that  I think that is an utterly misleading  statement it is a political statement  that is trying to give the impression  that there's ongoing conversations when  what they know is that every union today  asked a very simple question do you have  money now to alleviate the crisis the  answer was no every Union asked the  question do you have plans to do  something about the crisis in the Civil  Service and the answer was no and  therefore you know people can be invited  to meetings as often as they like but if  there's nothing available to about the  crisis it must mean that the government  is prepared to accept the disruption and  the poverty that they know that exists  okay let's talk about fair and  affordable because that's the phrase  that they keep coming up with the  government say that the unions have to  come up with a fair and affordable  um settlement  if there is no money and that is what  you say they keep telling you they have  no money what are you asking for and  what do you think is fair and affordable  so the first thing to say is if they say  there's no money available that is a lie  let's just call it what it is that is a  lie why do you say that because we all  know everybody knows there is money  available if they choose to make it  available we've seen in the Wales for  example at the Welsh government is  trying to find money from very stressed  resources to deal with the problems in  the Health Service it's a matter of  political priority this government was  prepared to borrow hundreds of billions  of pounds to give tax cuts to the  richest we're asking for a tiny fraction  of what they would have borrowed then so  what specifically what specifically are  you asking for because the argument is  if we keep borrowing keep borrowing  inflation will go up and we'll just  carry on in this situation the  government's argument is that they have  to cut inflation to sort it all out so  what specifically are you asking for in  terms of the paywrites that you that  you'd want so the first thing is the  government caused inflation let's be  clear about that the government's  mishandling of the economy double  people's mortgages there so that is on  the government and nothing can escape  that and pay raises to public sector  workers do not cause inflation and they  help fight the recession because our  members as we just heard from your  previous report spend the money they get  in the local economy they don't put it  in the Cayman Islands we've asked for a  10 pay raise to reflect the fact that if  we get less than the rate of inflation  we are poorer at the end of the year  than we were at the start but we've said  to the government don't duck the  argument by saying we can't afford 10 if  you say there is money available we will  talk to you night and day to try to  avert the strike they have to say that  they will find money we are prepared to  negotiate how much that is how it's  distributed but it has to happen now and  the government are effectively saying  the penny will be found
 

